WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.040
What is this going to be about?

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.360
Brainstormed some Ideas.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.840
Also we contacted concept artists.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.480
Started putting together some rough text

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.280
and rough imagery.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.200
Even actually went as far as casting

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.480
a mini, fake teaser for the peace.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.480
Which was mostly just cool graphics.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:29.760
and cool people posing on greenscreens

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.600
with guns.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.280
The whole point of all this was just to prove

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.880
we know how we wanna, kind of

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.800
play our characters and, not to mention

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.040
hey, look at how cool this stuff looks

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.600
if we did it in live action too!

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.800
There's a way Sam and I came at it.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.400
We're inspired by the multiplayer of Battlefeild 
-Yeah

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.240
I mean it has its single player campaign but,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.920
Realistically

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.460
The vast majority of people spend their time
playing the multiplayer.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.200
You just have to realize at its core

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.480
that is what you have to embrace

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.960
when you're gonna design a piece

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.560
based off a video game is the common experience.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.080
When you pitch a video game project

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.440
It's the hardest thing to figure out

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.240
alright what is the angle we're gonna hit this with,
you know.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.840
A lot of video game projects in the past I think

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.960
go wrong, because they are

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.480
A: Sticking to main characters in these story lines

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.920
and trying to recreate them.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.920
B: They're trying to 100% recreate.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.520
like settings or memorable/iconic things from the game.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.240
or they completely deviate all together

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.320
into a mass audience that has nothing to do with the game.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.400
Yeah! I mean, at that point.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.400
but the thing is that

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.280
at that point no matter which option you take

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.800
you're like pigeon holing youself

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.080
into these like

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.660
pre-writen, pre-made, you know, linear things that.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:34.840
honestly

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:36.600
players have already experienced.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.880
Well another issue too is
-Yeah.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.240
a lot of companies have this ideal of what their game is.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.600
that doesn't line up with what the public perceives it as.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.400
-Exactly.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.320
You know its like, they want their game to be this.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.840
So they make all their marketing like this.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.600
and they make all their official stuff like this but,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.500
the public doesn't see the game that way.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.160
and you get this weird mashup at times.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.140
and we've dealt with that when we've done

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.420
our various brand integrations on the channel.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.740
It's a very serious war game.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.500
there's no jokes, so to speak about it.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.300
Its, this is was, this is battle.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.460
It's 100% gritty and 100% serious.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.400
and then you flip that around

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.200
and you take a look at all the videos online of

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.260
gamers who are playing it. (ourselves included)

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:10.900
and usually it is...

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.780
you putting a bomb on some guys butt

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.200
when he's not looking and blowing him up.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.580
you know so thats

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.000
a lot closer to the actual common experience

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.760
and so you kind of have to recognize that

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.880
basically just have to balance both worlds a little bit.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.420
Everything in terms of just a general shooting style

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.300
like how we like to actually get things done.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.700
We have a certain way of operating on set

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.180
and so our goal was to take what we've learned

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.020
with low budget micro crew film making

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.340
and see how we can apply the best parts of that

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.860
to a larger film crew.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.600
Yeah, that's definitely an interesting challenge

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.260
and if you're running around

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.080
shooting with your friends

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.700
and you're getting millions of views on the internet

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.420
do you really need to change that?

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.680
If you move on to a different styled project

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.340
the answer is yes.
-Yes.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.060
*laughter*

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.480
Yup and more on that as you watch the behind the scenes.

